{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Selectively log network activity events by identity in AWS CloudTrail - AWS","context":"AWS has introduced enhanced event filtering for network activity events related to Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) endpoints in CloudTrail. This new feature allows customers to selectively log network activity events based on the identity of the IAM user making the API call. Users can configure selectors to log only specific events, such as access denied events, when the calling identity is not on a trusted list. This helps in reducing logging costs and filtering out routine traffic from trusted identities. The UserIdentity filtering feature can be used to focus on network activity event logging for security scenarios and detect potential data exfiltration attempts through VPC endpoints. Users can combine UserIdentity conditions with existing fields like eventName or vpcEndpointId for fine-grained control over what gets recorded. The feature is available via the AWS Management Console, AWS Command Line Interface, and AWS SDKs in all AWS Regions where CloudTrail network activity events are supported. For more information, visit the AWS CloudTrail user guide or read the AWS Blog on Network Activity Events","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/aws-cloudtrail-filter-useridentity-advance-selectors/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"aws-investigation","skill_description":"Investigates what is happening in an AWS account from evidence already recorded there. Not for capturing new data from running code (snapshots of function arguments on a live service, Dynamic Instrumentation), questions answered from the CloudWatch Omni context graph or Application Signals service map, or building or changing infrastructure. Covers reading deployment records instead of the newest stored version (completed AppConfig deployments, the version an alias points to, a stack's current template), following a request through every service's own logs (exact per-layer fields, unique failed requests in an alarm's breach window, failure boundary per event), and answering questions from the account's data (exact counts and breakdowns, coverage of the requested range, exact filters and joins, the rule behind failed or unmatched records, verbatim values). Use to triage an alarm, error spike or failed request, trace a request across services, or count, break down, extract or join records.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, appconfig, lambda, cloudformation, dynamodb, s3, athena, glue\nTasks: debug, investigate, query, troubleshoot\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: observability, operations","skill_name":"aws-investigation"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-cleanrooms","skill_description":"Troubleshoots and debugs AWS Clean Rooms collaboration issues related to IAM roles, S3 bucket policies, KMS keys, Lake Formation permissions, and CloudWatch logging for custom ML model training and inference jobs. Use when a customer reports permission failures, access errors, or log publishing issues in Clean Rooms.\n\nServices: cleanrooms, cleanrooms-ml, iam, s3, kms, lakeformation, cloudwatch\nTasks: debug, troubleshoot, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, analyst\nWorkload: data-collaboration, ml\n*","skill_name":"aws-cleanrooms"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify how CloudTrail LookupEvents Username filter behaves for assumed roles"}

